        // non-zero value, making a second seedMarket call revert. Keep ref controllable by the
        // fuzzer but bound it away from 0x0 to avoid the referral special case.
        vm.assume(initialDrugs > 0);
        vm.assume(ref != address(0));

        // Set up the scenario: first seedMarket succeeds, setting marketDrugs = initialDrugs > 0.
        // Then call seedMarket a second time with any amount, which must revert.
        vm.deal(address(this), 1 ether);
        c.seedMarket{value: 1 ether}(initialDrugs);

        // The second seedMarket must revert because marketDrugs != 0.
        // We capture the outcome with a try/catch and an ok flag.
        bool ok = true;
        try c.seedMarket(999) {
            // If we reach here the call did not revert.
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }

        // Assert the invariant: after a reverting seedMarket, initialized must remain unchanged
        // (it should still be true from the first successful call). The bug makes initialized
        // always true, which by itself wouldn't be caught here.
        // Instead we assert that the revert flag matches the absence of a state corruption:
        // the bug makes initialized true even when the call reverts, so the invariant is that
        // if the second call reverted, the contract state must still allow operations that depend
        // on initialized being true (it was already true). The bug breaks this by making
        // initialized true regardless of the revert, but that does NOT cause a state inconsistency
        // here because it was already true. We need a different invariant.

        // Re-read the instruction: the invariant must FAIL on the buggy version for SOME input.
        // After a REVERTING seedMarket, getFreeKilo() MUST revert because initialized should still
        // be whatever it was before (it was true). Wait—on the reference, initialized was true
        // so getFreeKilo() succeeds. On the buggy version, initialized is set true before the
        // require, so it also stays true after the revert. Both succeed. Not a distinction.

        // Alternative invariant: Call seedMarket(0) when marketDrugs > 0, it reverts. Before the
        // call initialized is true (from the first seed). After the revert, if we could somehow
        // reset initialized to false... impossible. The bug sets it true even on revert, so both
        // remain true. No difference.
    }
